8. Brackets:

    In C Programming, there are 3 main types of brackets you need to understand:

        () - Parentheses
        {} - Curly braces
        [] - Square brackets
        <> - Angle brackets

8.a Parentheses () - For conditions and functions

    8.a.1 Condition in IF and WHILE:

            int     age;

            age = 20;
            if (age >= 18)
            {
                printf("ADULT");
            }

        		the condition age >= 18 is inside parentheses.

    8.a.2 Functions:

            printf("Hello!");

        		Parentheses contain the arguments passed by the function.

    8.a.3 Calculations:

        int result;

        result = (5 + 3) * 2;

    			Result: 16, Parentheses make 5 + 3 happen first.

8.b Curly braces {} - For blocks of code

    8.b.1 Inside if statements:

        if (x > 10)
        {
            printf("Big");
            printf(" number);
        }

    			The braces group both printf statements into one block.

    8.b.2 Inside functions

        int main (void)
        {
            int     x;

            x = 10;
            return (0);
            }

        		The funcion's body starts at { and ends at }.

    8.b.3 Initializing arrays.

        		int     numbers[3] = {10, 20, 30};

    	The curly braces enclose the initial values of the array.

8.c Square bracets [] - For arrays.

    	Square brackets are used to declare and access their elements.

            	int numbers[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};

        	Here, [4] means the array can hold up to four integers.

    	To access individual elements:

                printf("%d", numbers[0]); // 10
                printf("%d", numbers[1]); // 20
                printf("%d", numbers[2]); // 30
                printf("%d", numbers[3]); // 40

        Important: Array indexes start at 0, not 1.

                10              20              30              40
                index 0         index 1         index 2         index 3
                numbers[0]      numbers[1]      numbers[2]      numbers[3]

        You can also use square brackets with strings

            char name[] = "Alex";
            printf("%c", name[0]); // A

8.d Angle brackets <> - For headers and comparison.

    Angle brackets have two common uses.

        8.d.1 Including standard library headers.

                #include <stdio.h>
                #include <unistd.h>

            The angle brackets tell the processor to search for these headers
            using its standard header-search rules.

        8.d.2 Comparing values

                if (a < b)
                {
                    printf("a is smaller")
                }

            Here < means less than. Similarly, > means greater than.

8.e All brackets together in one progeram


            int main (void)
            {
                int number[3] = {10, 20, 30};

                if (numbers[0] < numbers[1])
                {
                    printf("First number is smaller\n");
                }
                return (0);
            }


What each bracket does:

        <> in #include <stdio.h> - identifies the header to include.

        () in main(void) - contains the function's parameter list.

        {} after main(void) - contains the function body.

        [] in numbers[3] - declares an array with three elements.

        {} in {10, 20, 30} - initializes the array.

        () in if (...) - contains the condition.

        [] in numbers[0] and numbers[1] - accesses array elements.

        {} after the if condition - contains the statements to execute when the condition is true.

        () in printf(...) - contains the function arguments.


Remember this simple rule:

        () - Functions and conditions.

        {} - Blocks of code and initializers.

        [] - Arrays and element access.

        <> - Header inclusion and comparison operators, depending on context.

